// @corpus: platform
// @id: PLATFORM-build
// @category: platform — tracked build inputs
// @charter: §9.3, §7.9
// @milestone: E10
//
// The operations a build-time page may use to read its inputs.
//
// Architect ruling, 2026-08-10:
//
//   allowed at build time:
//       declared/tracked build inputs
//
//   forbidden or specially modeled:
//       arbitrary fs reads, current time, randomness, network state,
//       undeclared environment
//
// `include_markdown` was called by `examples/accepted/A-013` and declared
// nowhere. The page claimed build-time determinism while reading a file that
// nothing tracked, and the claim held only because the call named nothing —
// so the placement solver had no effect to constrain and put the page in every
// world including the browser.

module build

// The resource a build input read is scoped to. A platform concept, declared
// here rather than in the application's domain: `build.input.read<WorkspaceFile>`
// is authority over the workspace, and a program cannot widen it by declaring
// its own type of the same name.
type WorkspaceFile = WorkspaceFile { path: String }

// A workspace file, read at build time and recorded as a build input.
//
// **The path must be statically knowable.** The ruling is explicit that dynamic
// arbitrary path lookup stays closed until there is a concrete need: a build
// input the build graph cannot name before running is not a tracked input.
// Enforcing that is a checker, and it is recorded in `docs/NEXT.md` rather than
// implied by this signature.
fn include_markdown(path: String) -> String !{ build.input.read<WorkspaceFile> } {
    ""
}
